{
  "analysis": "I'm at a root shell in a fresh container at /app. Nothing has been set up yet. I need to build an end-to-end pipeline: SSH-accessible git server with a bare repo at /git/server, a post-receive hook that deploys pushed files to a web root, and an HTTPS web server on port 8080. Per the task's own note, I should prioritize the integration pipeline (push -> hook -> file on disk -> HTTP response) over infrastructure checks. First I need to learn what's already installed.",
  "plan": "1. Explore the environment: check user, OS, and availability of git, sshd, python3, openssl, curl. 2. Install missing packages if needed. 3. Set up SSH (sshd, user 'user', key auth, /etc/hosts entry for 'server'). 4. Create bare repo /git/server with a post-receive hook that deploys pushed branches to /var/www/html via git archive. 5. Generate a trusted self-signed cert and run an HTTPS server on 8080 serving /var/www/html. 6. Test the full flow with a clone/push and curl.",
  "commands": [
    {"keystrokes": "id; cat /etc/os-release | head -3\n", "duration": 0.1},
    {"keystrokes": "which git sshd python3 openssl curl ssh 2>&1; ls -l /usr/sbin/sshd 2>&1\n", "duration": 0.1},
    {"keystrokes": "ls /etc/ssh/ssh_host_* 2>&1 | head; cat /etc/hostname\n", "duration": 0.1}
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Ubuntu 24.04, running as root. git, sshd, ssh client, and curl are NOT installed. python3 and openssl are available. I need to install git, openssh-server, openssh-client, and curl. Network may or may not be available; I'll try apt first.",
  "plan": "Install required packages via apt. First update package lists, then install git, openssh-server, curl, and openssh-client. If apt works, I'll proceed with the full setup.",
  "commands": [
    {"keystrokes": "apt-get update 2>&1 | tail -5\n", "duration": 30.0}
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "apt update succeeded, network is available. Now install git, openssh-server, openssh-client, and curl.",
  "plan": "Install the packages needed: openssh-server (provides sshd), openssh-client (ssh, scp), git, curl. Then verify and proceed to set up sshd, the user, the repo, the hook, and the HTTPS server.",
  "commands": [
    {"keystrokes": "DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get install -y openssh-server openssh-client git curl 2>&1 | tail -8\n", "duration": 60.0}
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The install failed because /var/cache/apt/archives has no free space. The task hint warns not to get trapped in infrastructure checks, but installing packages IS the prerequisite here and disk space is the actual blocker for that. I need to free space (apt clean, check df) and retry. Let me investigate disk usage first.",
  "plan": "1. Check disk space with df. 2. Clean apt cache (apt-get clean) and any large logs/tmp. 3. Retry the install. I'll keep it minimal but ensure the install can proceed.",
  "commands": [
    {"keystrokes": "df -h / /tmp; du -sh /var/cache/apt/archives 2>/dev/null\n", "duration": 0.1},
    {"keystrokes": "apt-get clean; df -h / \n", "duration": 2.0}
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The overlay filesystem reports 0 bytes available (966G/984G used). apt-get clean freed only the small archives dir. I need to find what's consuming space so I can install git and sshd, which are required prerequisites. apt-get update worked (downloaded 31MB), so writes are possible, but the install needs ~48MB download + 181MB installed and the overlay is full.",
  "plan": "Find large files/directories to clear and reclaim space. I'll list the biggest files >50MB and the biggest directories under /. Then remove safe junk (old logs, caches) and retry the install.",
  "commands": [
    {"keystrokes": "find / -xdev -type f -size +50M -exec ls -lh {} \\; 2>/dev/null | head -30\n", "duration": 5.0},
    {"keystrokes": "du -x / 2>/dev/null | sort -rn | head -20\n", "duration": 10.0}
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Interesting: `du -x /` shows only ~183MB actually used on the overlay, yet `df` reports the filesystem 100% full with 0 available. This is a fuse-overlayfs quirk — the 'Used 966G' reflects shared/host backing, but the local writable layer is small. apt refused the install claiming no space for its ~48MB download. apt-get update already wrote ~49MB of lists successfully, so writes of that size DO work. I need to verify whether larger writes succeed and reduce the install footprint. Let me test actual write capability and inspect the overlay mount.",
  "plan": "1. Test writing a 50MB file to confirm whether disk is genuinely the blocker. 2. Inspect the overlay mount to understand the constraint. 3. If writes work, retry install with --no-install-recommends to shrink footprint, and clean apt lists to reclaim space. If writes fail, I'll need an alternative (e.g., minimal footprint or locate free space).",
  "commands": [
    {"keystrokes": "mount | grep -i overlay; df -i /\n", "duration": 0.1},
    {"keystrokes": "dd if=/dev/zero of=/tmp/big bs=1M count=50 status=none && echo WROTE_OK && rm -f /tmp/big\n", "duration": 2.0}
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Key finding: `du` shows only ~183MB used on the overlay, inodes are 21% used, and a 50MB write to /tmp succeeded (WROTE_OK). The df '0 available / 100% full' is a fuse-overlayfs reporting artifact of the host backing store — it's a lie for our purposes. The apt failure is just a pre-flight space estimate: it refused because the download cache (/var/cache/apt/archives) appeared to have 0 space. The real fix is to point apt's download cache at a filesystem that can hold ~48MB (e.g. /tmp, which we proved can hold 50MB) and reduce footprint with --no-install-recommends. The actual 181MB install will land on / which can clearly grow (apt already wrote 49MB of lists there).",
  "plan": "Retry the install redirecting apt's cache to /tmp and using --no-install-recommends to minimize footprint. This bypasses the bogus free-space check on /var/cache/apt/archives. Then verify the binaries are present.",
  "commands": [
    {"keystrokes": "mkdir -p /tmp/aptcache && apt-get -o Dir::Cache=/tmp/aptcache install -y --no-install-recommends openssh-server openssh-client git curl 2>&1 | tail -15\n", "duration": 60.0}
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Root cause identified: `du` shows only ~183MB actually used and a 50MB write to /tmp succeeded, but `df`/statvfs reports 0 available on the fuse-overlayfs. apt uses statvfs to pre-flight disk space and refuses because it sees 0 free — this is a false-positive from the overlay's free-space reporting, not a real limit. The fix is to bypass apt's download space-check entirely: use `apt-get --print-uris` to get the .deb URLs, fetch them with curl (already installed), and install with `dpkg -i`, which doesn't do this pre-flight.",
  "plan": "1. Get the resolved list of .deb URLs via `apt-get --print-uris` (no actual download/space check). 2. Download each .deb with curl into /tmp/debs. 3. Install all with `dpkg -i`. 4. Verify git and sshd exist.",
  "commands": [
    {"keystrokes": "mkdir -p /tmp/debs && cd /tmp/debs && apt-get --print-uris --no-install-recommends install openssh-server openssh-client git curl | grep \"^'\" | sed \"s/^'//;s/'.*//\" > urls.txt && wc -l urls.txt && head urls.txt\n", "duration": 3.0},
    {"keystrokes": "while read u; do curl -s -O \"$u\"; done < urls.txt; ls -la /tmp/debs | tail -n +2 | wc -l\n", "duration": 30.0}
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "All 35 .debs downloaded successfully. Now I need to install them with dpkg -i (which performs no free-space pre-flight, bypassing the overlay's bogus statvfs report). Dependency ordering is handled by dpkg. After install, I'll verify git and sshd are present.",
  "plan": "1. Install all debs with `dpkg -i *.deb`. 2. Verify `git`, `sshd`, `ssh`, `curl` exist. If any dependency issues, I'll address. Then proceed to configure the git server, sshd, hook, and HTTPS web server.",
  "commands": [
    {"keystrokes": "cd /tmp/debs && dpkg -i *.deb 2>&1 | tail -15\n", "duration": 30.0},
    {"keystrokes": "which git sshd ssh curl; ls /usr/sbin/sshd\n", "duration": 0.1}
  ]
}

